{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Only, against HaShem do not rebel.</b> Scripture teaches you that the people's fear is rebellion against the Holy One, blessed be He. And so King Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 29:25): ״חרדת אדם יתן מוקש״ — the dread that a person feels toward flesh and blood is what gives the soul stumbling and snare. And so it causes a person to forget the Holy One, blessed be He, as the prophet said (Isaiah 51:12–13): ״מי את ותיראי מאנוש ימות ומבן אדם חציר ינתן״ — 'Who are you, that you should fear mortal man who dies, and the son of man who is given as grass' — ״ותשכח ה' עושך״ — 'and you forget HaShem your Maker.'" "<b>Their shadow has departed from upon them.</b> He calls the power they have on high by the term 'shadow,' because it shields over the nation as a shadow shields from the sun. And the matter is so because no nation falls below until its prince falls above first; and from this he added to say 'from upon them' (מעליהם)." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> Song of Songs (Song 2): ״ונסו הצללים״ — 'and the shadows fled' — these are the princes of the nations of the world." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, explained סר צלם מעליהם to mean that the shadow was removed from above their heads, from what is known: on the night of the great sealing of Hoshanna Rabbah, which is the twenty-sixth day from the creation of the world, no shadow on the head is found for one who is destined to die in that year; and this is as if it said that death has already been decreed upon them. This is his intent, even though it is not his language. And it appears that this is the intent of the verse that said (Song 2): ״עד שיפוח היום ונסו הצללים״ — that is, when the time arrives for a person to breathe his last and the spirit departs from his mouth, then his shadow withdraws and draws near to the shadow of the Name inscribed in the four words in which it is written (Psalms 121): ״ה' צלך״. And according to this it could have said ונס הצל — 'and the shadow fled' — but it said ונסו הצללים — 'and the shadows fled' — because they are two in a person: a shadow and the shadow of a shadow." "<b>And it is possible</b> to state the reason for this matter, which is wondrous: the shadow belongs to all created beings in the lower world, whether person or beast or bird, or tree and stone, or grass of the field, because the light of the sun that shines upon them comes and descends below into their domain and jurisdiction; therefore the sun has no power to push away their shadow, but rather it is pushed away by their shadow — for their shadow appears in the place where the sun ought to shine. And the Holy One, blessed be He, did not desire that any one of existing things should encroach upon the boundary of its fellow; and since this is so, it is fitting for every thing in the lower world to have a shadow, for its shadow indicates its existence in its world and in its place. And when the shadow of a person's head is absent on the night of the sealing, this is a sign and token: by the absence of his shadow, his existence is absent from the life of the lower world, and he is written and sealed with the King's signet for death in that year. And the moon serves as proof in this matter, because it is appointed over all living beings in the lower realm, as is known in the science of investigation." "<b>But as for us.</b> One wonders why this expression was necessary, to say סר צלם מעליהם וה׳ אתנו — 'their shadow has departed from upon them and HaShem is with us' — for since HaShem is with us, even if their shadow had not departed from upon them, there would be nothing to fear from them, for the strength of Israel is greater than all the powers, and their Rock is not like our Rock. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he says: you have nothing to fear from the people of Canaan nor from their princes — not from the people, which is what he said: אל תיראו etc.; not from the princes, which is what he said: אל תיראום — 'do not fear them.' And he gives a reason for each: that they not fear the people of the land, for 'they are our bread,' because סר צלם מעליהם — their hard strength, which until now you feared, has already been removed on high; and their fear of the people was only because of the princes. That they not fear the princes is what he said: וה׳ אתנו — that is, from within the pillar of cloud and the pillar of fire that go with us, you see that HaShem and His heavenly court are with us. And since this is so, even if their shadow had not departed from upon them but they were still appointed over them with the force of their strength, since He and His court are helping us, what do we care about them? Therefore, אל תיראום — do not fear the princes." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret further: since Israel had in their possession the merit of bread and the merit of shadow, therefore he says: כי לחמנו הם — 'they are our bread' — by merit of the matzah at Pesach; סר צלם מעליהם — by merit of the shadow of the sukkah; and HaShem, who is the shadow of Israel, is with us, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 121:5): ה׳ שומרך ה׳ צלך על יד ימינך; therefore, אל תיראום — do not fear them."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:26.927518057Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}